1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,205
What is the fair price of a financial option? For decades, this was one

2
00:00:06,305 --> 00:00:13,132
of the most difficult questions in finance. An option is a contract giving you

3
00:00:13,232 --> 00:00:18,728
the right, but not the obligation, to buy or sell an asset at a

4
00:00:18,828 --> 00:00:25,388
specific price, called the strike price, on a specific date. But how do you

5
00:00:25,488 --> 00:00:31,872
put a price on a right? This is the problem that three economists solved,

6
00:00:31,972 --> 00:00:36,668
and in doing so, they transformed Wall Street forever.

7
00:00:37,768 --> 00:00:43,995
Let's visualize a simple call option. The horizontal axis is the

8
00:00:44,095 --> 00:00:50,223
stock price at expiration. The vertical axis is your payoff. If

9
00:00:50,323 --> 00:00:56,353
the stock price finishes below the strike price, the option is

10
00:00:56,453 --> 00:01:02,581
worthless. But if it rises above the strike, your payoff grows,

11
00:01:02,681 --> 00:01:08,316
dollar for dollar. This is the famous hockey stick payoff.

12
00:01:09,416 --> 00:01:16,229
In the early 1970s, Fischer Black, Myron Scholes, and Robert Merton

13
00:01:16,329 --> 00:01:23,246
achieved a major breakthrough. They didn't just find a formula; they

14
00:01:23,346 --> 00:01:30,779
discovered a fundamental principle of finance. Their insight was that you

15
00:01:30,879 --> 00:01:38,725
could perfectly hedge an option by continuously trading the underlying stock.

16
00:01:38,825 --> 00:01:45,639
By creating a riskless portfolio, they could price the option using

17
00:01:45,739 --> 00:01:53,068
the risk-free rate. This idea, known as no-arbitrage, earned Scholes and

18
00:01:53,168 --> 00:01:59,466
Merton the Nobel Prize in Economics in 1997. Fischer Black had

19
00:01:59,566 --> 00:02:04,420
passed away, but his name lives on in the model.

20
00:02:05,520 --> 00:02:10,669
The Black-Scholes model is built on a set of elegant,

21
00:02:10,769 --> 00:02:17,603
albeit strict, assumptions. First, the stock price follows a geometric

22
00:02:17,703 --> 00:02:24,438
Brownian motion with constant drift and volatility. Second, there are

23
00:02:24,538 --> 00:02:30,877
no transaction costs or taxes. Third, the risk-free interest rate

24
00:02:30,977 --> 00:02:36,622
is constant and known. Fourth, the stock pays no dividends

25
00:02:36,722 --> 00:02:42,862
during the option's life. And fifth, trading is continuous, and

26
00:02:42,962 --> 00:02:50,886
short-selling is allowed. These assumptions create a perfect, frictionless market

27
00:02:50,986 --> 00:02:54,452
where a unique price can be derived.

28
00:02:55,552 --> 00:03:00,746
At the heart of the model is the idea that stock prices

29
00:03:00,846 --> 00:03:07,387
wander randomly, like a particle in a fluid. This is called geometric

30
00:03:07,487 --> 00:03:14,703
Brownian motion. While the exact path is unpredictable, the logarithm of the

31
00:03:14,803 --> 00:03:21,729
price is normally distributed. This means we can model the probability of

32
00:03:21,829 --> 00:03:28,371
future prices using a bell curve. Let's simulate a few possible paths

33
00:03:28,471 --> 00:03:31,548
a stock might take over one year.

34
00:03:33,148 --> 00:03:43,040
From these assumptions, they derived a closed-form solution: the Black-Scholes formula. For a

35
00:03:43,140 --> 00:03:49,380
European call option, it is C equals S times N of d1, minus

36
00:03:49,480 --> 00:03:55,075
K times e to the power of negative rT, times N of d2.

37
00:03:55,175 --> 00:04:01,522
Here, S is the current stock price, K is the strike price, r

38
00:04:01,622 --> 00:04:08,398
is the risk-free rate, T is the time to expiration, and sigma is

39
00:04:08,498 --> 00:04:16,779
the volatility. N of x is the cumulative distribution function of the standard

40
00:04:16,879 --> 00:04:25,805
normal distribution. The terms d1 and d2 combine these variables into a standardized

41
00:04:25,905 --> 00:04:31,608
measure of how far the stock price is from the strike.

42
00:04:32,708 --> 00:04:39,129
Let's bring this formula to life. This 3D surface shows the call

43
00:04:39,229 --> 00:04:45,650
price as a function of two variables: the current stock price on

44
00:04:45,750 --> 00:04:52,171
the x-axis, and the time to expiration on the y-axis. Notice how

45
00:04:52,271 --> 00:04:59,303
the option value increases as the stock price rises. It also increases

46
00:04:59,403 --> 00:05:06,945
with more time to expiration, because more time means more uncertainty, and

47
00:05:07,045 --> 00:05:13,569
more potential for the stock to move favorably. As time runs out,

48
00:05:13,669 --> 00:05:20,599
the surface drops toward the intrinsic payoff we saw earlier. This is

49
00:05:20,699 --> 00:05:24,064
the Black-Scholes model in motion.

50
00:05:25,164 --> 00:05:31,740
The model also gives us the Greeks: Delta, Gamma, Theta, Vega, and

51
00:05:31,840 --> 00:05:38,720
Rho. These are the risk management tools of the options trader. Delta

52
00:05:38,820 --> 00:05:45,194
measures the change in option price for a one-dollar move in the

53
00:05:45,294 --> 00:05:52,578
stock. Gamma measures how fast Delta changes. Theta measures the decay of

54
00:05:52,678 --> 00:06:00,570
the option's value as time passes. Vega measures sensitivity to volatility. And

55
00:06:00,670 --> 00:06:08,966
Rho measures sensitivity to interest rates. Together, these Greeks allow traders to

56
00:06:09,066 --> 00:06:14,024
build and hedge complex portfolios with precision.

57
00:06:15,130 --> 00:06:19,666
Of course, the real world is not as perfect as

58
00:06:19,766 --> 00:06:26,720
the model's assumptions. Markets can jump, volatility is not constant,

59
00:06:26,820 --> 00:06:33,068
and transaction costs exist. The 1987 stock market crash, known

60
00:06:33,168 --> 00:06:39,014
as Black Monday, exposed some of these limitations when the

61
00:06:39,114 --> 00:06:45,665
model's prices diverged dramatically from reality. This led to the

62
00:06:45,765 --> 00:06:53,928
development of more advanced models, like stochastic volatility and jump-diffusion

63
00:06:54,028 --> 00:07:01,284
models. But the Black-Scholes model remains the foundation, the benchmark

64
00:07:01,384 --> 00:07:05,718
against which all other models are measured.

65
00:07:06,864 --> 00:07:13,419
The Black-Scholes model transformed finance. It gave the world a common

66
00:07:13,519 --> 00:07:20,074
language for pricing risk. While its assumptions don't always hold, its

67
00:07:20,174 --> 00:07:27,386
legacy is undeniable. It earned a Nobel Prize, spawned a multi-trillion-dollar

68
00:07:27,486 --> 00:07:34,978
derivatives industry, and forever changed how we understand uncertainty. The fair

69
00:07:35,078 --> 00:07:39,478
price of an option is no longer a mystery. It is

70
00:07:39,578 --> 00:07:44,540
a formula, a model, and a monument to human ingenuity.

